--TEST--
`Core\Serialize` names what it refused: a callable has no meaning across a copy boundary, and a payload that is not Novis's own format is refused whole — `rule:classes/graph-copy` and `rule:classes/serialize-is-a-closed-format`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2: "refuses what has no meaning on the other side" — a callable captures a
// heap and a scope, so it is refused *naming the offending value* rather than
// degraded into a stub or silently dropped. It is a `LogicError` and not a
// `ParseError`: the value was built by this program, so it is a bug in it.
var $fn = fn(): int => 1;
try {
    Core\Serialize::encode($fn);
    echo "encoded a callable\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// § 3: a payload that does not carry Novis's format marker and version is
// refused outright — not best-effort parsed, not partially accepted. The
// refusal is the assertion, not what any field of a result holds.
try {
    Core\Serialize::decode("O:4:\"Slot\":1:{s:1:\"n\";i:7;}" as bytes);
    echo "decoded PHP's own wire format\n";
} catch (ParseError $foreign) {
    echo $foreign->message, "\n";
}

// § 3 again, one byte in: our marker, a version this build does not read. The
// bound is asserted from both sides — the line above shares no bytes with the
// format at all, and this one shares all but the version.
try {
    Core\Serialize::decode("NVS\x1b\x63\x00" as bytes);
    echo "decoded another build's format\n";
} catch (ParseError $version) {
    echo $version->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Serialize::encode(): a callable captures variables from the code that created it, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary
Core\Serialize::decode(): the payload does not carry Novis's serialization marker
Core\Serialize::decode(): the payload is format version 99, and this build reads 1
